#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088568} :activity/id #uuid "738132c2-94a1-5f53-8c93-d33a4d0655d5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051081 :topic/id #uuid "7fa30ad3-9041-5912-9f1e-90bd24bebbe8" :topic/math-academy-id 850} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088568 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088910 :tutorial/content "When an ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis, we must have $y = 0.$ So, to find the $x$-intercepts of an ellipse, we substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$\n\nFor example, let's find the $x$-intercepts of the ellipse given by the equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-3)^2}{4} + y^2 = 1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=0$ into the equation and solving the resulting equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+{0}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =3\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =1,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(1,0)$ and $(5,0).$ We can verify this by plotting the graph:\n\n![](images/8c/8c14d3e9c1738c41bf97a8e715839f53fd073ac0a502adf0dec594d6e27ef2a0.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "3b8c37af-7386-5b3e-9594-49f3b34d1830" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3410 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4ae790e0-18d0-5862-bc48-9e27aa122785" :step/math-academy-id 19217 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088583}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088569 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087724 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087525 :question/id #uuid "58d92951-d993-50ec-806c-802c627ada96" :question/math-academy-id "e-5213"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049250}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292736} {:db/id 